## The Origin and the Mother

**Source:** Lǎozǐ, *Dào Dé Jīng* 道德經, Chapter 52

When the cosmos first opens, it is the mother of all. Knowing the mother lets us know the children, and guarding that mother keeps us safe. The chapter urges a quiet, inward attention that yields strength and clarity.

---

### Original (Traditional Chinese)

天下有始，以為天下母。
既知其母，復知其子，既知其子，復守其母，沒其不殆。
塞其兌，閉其門，終身不勤。
開其兌，濟其事，終身不救。
見小曰明，守柔曰強。
用其光，復歸其明，無遺身殃；
是為習常。

### Pratibhā Translation

1. When the world has a beginning, it is the [天下] (tiānxià) mother of all. 2. Knowing that mother makes us know the children; knowing the children makes us guard the mother, and in that guarding we are free from danger. 3. Close the mouth and shut the nostrils, and you will never be forced to labor. Open the mouth and breathe for your affairs, and you will never be safe. 4. Seeing the small is [明] (míng) – clarity; guarding the soft is [強] (qiáng) – strength. 5. Use the light wisely, return to its brightness, and you will keep your body free of harm; this is the habit of the constant.

### Pratibhā Commentary

This chapter asserts that the Tao is the primordial mother of all things, and that by recognizing and safeguarding that mother one achieves safety. The contested move is the paradoxical injunction to close the mouth and nostrils to avoid labor, versus opening them to act, suggesting that action without intention is perilous while silence preserves the Tao. The text does not merely describe a method; it claims that the very act of guarding the mother—of aligning oneself with the natural order—constitutes a living practice of the Tao. It is a call to internal stillness that paradoxically yields external strength, a theme that echoes throughout the Tao Te Ching.

### Key Terms

**天下 (tiānxià)** — the world or universe -> the mother of all -> the source of all existence
**明 (míng)** — clarity or insight -> the perception of the small -> the light that reveals
**強 (qiáng)** — strength or power -> the guarding of the soft -> the power of yielding
**道 (dào)** — the Way -> the primordial principle -> the source of all being

### Cross-Tradition Resonances

**chapter 1 (the Tao that can be named is not the eternal Tao):** Both chapters start with the Tao as a source, but chapter 52 frames it as a mother rather than an abstract principle *Divergence:* chapter 1 speaks of the ineffable, while chapter 52 gives a concrete relational image
**chapter 33 (the Tao is the mother of all):** Both use the mother metaphor, but chapter 33 emphasizes the Tao’s generative role, whereas chapter 52 focuses on the protective duty of the child
**chapter 56 (the Tao is the source of all things):** Both identify the Tao as the origin, but chapter 56 is more descriptive, chapter 52 is prescriptive about guarding the mother

### Practice (Abhyāsa)

Sit in stillness for ten minutes each morning, closing your mouth and nostrils in a gentle breath, and observe how the quietness protects you from the day’s distractions.

